  "articleSummary": "The article argues against the common practice of committing to a single language model for all tasks, framing it as expensive and suboptimal. It proposes a routing architecture using Mastra, where a lightweight supervisor agent delegates work to specialized models—for example, using a premium model for coding, a long-context model for document analysis, and a cheap model for classification. The tone is a practical tutorial with a critical edge, employing marriage and single-hire metaphors to emphasize the inefficiency of “marrying” one model. The intended audience is engineering teams building LLM-based applications, with a focus on cost efficiency, quality matching, and explicit resilience patterns.",
